//
// Library.KernelABI.ailang
// Linux kernel-module shim ABI symbol table.
// Location: Librarys/Compiler/Link/OS/Linux/Library.KernelABI.ailang
// Import:   LibraryImport.Compiler.Link.OS.Linux.KernelABI
//
// PURPOSE:
//   When compiling with target_kind = "kmod", syscall-like primitives
//   cannot be emitted as `syscall` instructions — there is no userspace
//   syscall ABI inside a kernel module. Instead, calls are routed to the
//   C shim's stable `ail_*` surface via PLT32 relocations.
//
// MAPPING (syscall name -> shim symbol, mirrors kernel_module/shim/ail_shim.h):
//   WRITE    -> ail_printk            (kernel has no write(2); use printk)
//   MMAP     -> ail_kmalloc           (closest kernel analogue)
//   MUNMAP   -> ail_kfree
//   (no mapping) -> compile error in kmod mode
//
// NOT ALL SYSCALLS HAVE KERNEL ANALOGUES:
//   execve, fork, open-by-path — these are userspace-only concepts.
//   If payload calls them, analyzer emits a specific diagnostic naming
//   the offending call and suggesting the nearest kernel primitive
//   (or that the source is not portable to kmod targets).
//
// EXTRA SHIM-ONLY FUNCTIONS (no syscall counterpart):
//   ail_ktime_get_ns, ail_mutex_init/lock/unlock, ail_atomic_*,
//   ail_alloc_chrdev_region, ail_cdev_add, ail_ioremap, ail_read{b,w,l},
//   ail_write{b,w,l}, ail_copy_{to,from}_user, ail_msleep, ail_udelay.
//   These are reachable only through the future ExternalKernelFunction
//   declarations in Librarys/OS/Linux/Library.KernelShim.ailang.
//
// STATUS: Scaffold. The syscall->shim map fills in as kmod test programs
// exercise new primitives; start minimal (WRITE only) and grow.
